    "executiveSummary": "Modern institutions crave the comforting illusion of certainty. Leaders commission massive spreadsheets to calculate precise odds for unpredictable events. This is akin to using a microscope to navigate a sudden sea squall.\n\nIn well-defined games like roulette, known risks can be mathematically optimized. In real life, volatile markets, and emergent crises, true uncertainty reigns supreme. Trying to optimize with complex models only produces fragile, overfitted strategies that shatter on contact with reality.\n\nFlawed incentive structures compound this systemic failure across industries. When managers fear personal blame more than operational inefficiency, they practice defensive decision-making. They choose the defensible second-best option over the optimal move simply to protect their careers.\n\nA corporate committee will hire an expensive consulting firm not for strategic clarity, but to buy an insurance policy against future blame. True leadership demands removing these misaligned incentives, enforcing skin in the game, and fostering psychological safety so teams execute sound choices rather than defensive maneuvers.\n\nTo command chaotic environments, decision-makers must deploy fast-and-frugal heuristics. Like an elite outfielder catching a fly ball, professionals do not calculate wind resistance and parabolic calculus; they fix their gaze on the ball and adjust their running speed to maintain a constant angle. In business and medicine, transparent decision trees and simple rules consistently outperform opaque algorithmic black boxes.\n\nStripping away non-essential noise exposes the vital variables that actually drive performance under pressure.",
