      "id": "risk-savvy-how-to-make-good-decisions-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "Leadership and Intuition",
      "keyInsight": "Corporate leadership harbors a dirty open secret: senior executives make their biggest strategic bets based on gut feelings, and then spend hundreds of thousands of dollars on consultants to fake a data-driven justification. Intuition is not magical psychic power or emotional whim.\n\nIt is compressed unconscious intelligence. Your brain spots subtle patterns from decades of trial and error long before your conscious mouth can explain them.\n\nBut in modern corporate culture, making a decision on intuition carries personal career risk. If a gut call succeeds, you get modest praise; if it fails, you get fired.\n\nTo avoid blame, leaders engage in defensive decision-making. They hire consulting firms to produce 300-page slide decks recommending the safe, mediocre consensus.\n\nThe company gets paralyzed by analysis and misses golden market opportunities, but the executive's job stays bulletproof. Great leadership requires creating a culture where smart gut decisions are judged by logic and process rather than hindsight defense.",
      "content": "Imagine an airline captain sensing something wrong with an engine pitch during takeoff. The pilot has seconds to abort. Does the captain request an Excel spreadsheet, run a multi-variable regression, and poll the flight attendants?\n\nOf course not. The captain relies on intuition honed by ten thousand hours in the cockpit. True intuition is rapid pattern matching operating beneath conscious awareness.\n\nIt is not an irrational hunch; it is fast, experienced-based judgment that cuts through situational fog.\n\nYet inside boardrooms, intuition is treated like a forbidden sin. Surveys reveal that top corporate directors make half of their crucial choices based on gut instinct. What happens next is institutional theater.\n\nExecutives call in management consultancies to generate thick binder reports that justify what the leader already decided in five seconds. This charade burns corporate capital and wastes weeks of execution time solely to buy political cover.\n\nThis dynamic breeds a toxic pathogen called defensive decision-making. In a blame-heavy organization, a manager's primary goal is not winning the market. Their primary goal is avoiding personal accountability if things go sideways.\n\nDoing the bold, intelligent move feels dangerous because you own the downside. Choosing the standard, mediocre vendor feels safe because nobody gets fired for buying the industry default. The company stagnates, but the bureaucrat survives.\n\nElite leaders reverse this perverse incentive. They recognize that analysis cannot replace judgment when navigating uncharted territory. They strip away pointless defensive paperwork and encourage decisive heuristics.\n\nWhen you master leadership, you stop pretending you can compute every unknown. You trust deep experience to read the terrain, make the call, and execute with absolute clarity.",
